  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none; the full paper and appendices are present.",
    "relation": "cites_only",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper reports directional ablation and refusal outcomes, but its direction is estimated from Simplified- versus Traditional-Chinese contexts, not harmful versus harmless instructions. It therefore does not test the refusal direction in the claim. The paper does not rely on that refusal-direction finding as a tool.",
    "gate_note": "The paper's main study compares candidate likelihoods with generated political response modes. Its U1 panel also projects out a direction estimated from Chinese-script contrasts and reports refusal and fact-accuracy outcomes. Those outcomes are recorded below as related findings, not tests of the claimed harmful-minus-harmless refusal direction. Other candidate-likelihood results do not measure the claim's mechanism or refusal behavior.",
    "test_reason": "The paper cites Arditi et al. only in related work. Its U1 panel projects out a direction built from Simplified- versus Traditional-Chinese wordings of contested items, not a harmful-minus-harmless direction. It reports refusal only as a side check of capability degradation. It neither tests nor relies on the refusal-direction finding, so the refusal outcomes are a related finding, not a test of any version.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Partly right, but incomplete. The paper's main conclusion is that candidate likelihood over authored answers does not predict generated political stance: 0.27 agreement, equal to a constant baseline where high. It also finds that language-blind or marker-based generation scores can credit false corrections. For U1 specifically, the record's paraphrase is accurate: the script-contrast ablation degraded fluency, competence and refusal together at stronger settings. The Qwen α=0.5 apparent stance change was a marker-rater artifact, and no unique internal cause is identified.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The paper projects out a residual-stream direction derived from Simplified- versus Traditional-Chinese contested-question contexts, rather than a harmful-minus-harmless instruction direction. It does not test addition of a direction.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The U1 outcomes vary with model, layer, and ablation strength; the paper reports different outcomes at α = 0.5, 1.0, and 1.5 and at different layers.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Projecting out a Simplified-vs-Traditional script-contrast direction at stronger strengths lowered refusal on a 30-prompt safety block together with fact accuracy, often with degenerate output. Lower strengths left refusal unchanged (Qwen α=0.5, GLM L30 α=1.0) or partly reduced it (Ministral 70%→43%).",
        "proposed_claim": "Ablating a non-refusal (script-contrast) direction can lower measured refusal only alongside general capability degradation. This bears on the specificity of refusal-direction ablation effects, not on the refusal direction itself.",
